      },
      "lineNbr": 4750,
      "author": {
        "id": 1106949
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-01-30T19:25:49Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "A unit is needed either in the name or in the comment.\n\nAlso, it\u0027ll be helpful to mention it\u0027s derived from reboot_timeout_sec of struct ec_params_hang_detect (or actually derive it by member_size).",
      "parentUuid": "3e0a355d_2de939cd",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 4750,
        "startChar": 8,
        "endLine": 4750,
        "endChar": 34
      },
      "revId": "861ab4bffcf8847cf5277ed5b722d6ed445365aa",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "c5502c68_66591b90",
        "filename": "include/ec_commands.h",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 4750,
      "author": {
        "id": 1442602
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-01-31T11:00:47Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This is the maximum value uint16_t reboot_timeout_sec can hold. yes, it can be long, but It is not as harmful as setting min timeout to low. I would keep it as the real timeout is much lower and set only once.\n\nI have discussed it also with Rad and the other approach he proposed is to get rid of EC_HANG_DETECT_MAX_TIMEOUT at all, as it is only used as a limiter on the kernel side and in the kernel module set explicitly the `max_timeout \u003d UINT16_MAX;` then we wouldn\u0027t drop this CL;",
      "parentUuid": "58b22735_75068240",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 4750,
        "startChar": 35,
        "endLine": 4750,
        "endChar": 40
      },
      "revId": "861ab4bffcf8847cf5277ed5b722d6ed445365aa",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "50b1544a_10228eb6",
        "filename": "include/ec_commands.h",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 4750,
      "author": {
        "id": 1106949
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-01-31T18:17:48Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e get rid of EC_HANG_DETECT_MAX_TIMEOUT at all, as it is only used as a limiter on the kernel side\n\nIt makes sense but you probably want to use member_size() instead of UINT16_MAX.\n\nI still vote for setting EC_HANG_DETECT_MAX_TIMEOUT to some sensible value. Without an upper limit, a bug setting a large timeout (e.g. 1000 sec) will be obscure. With an upper bound (e.g. 100 sec), we\u0027ll be able to catch more errors.",
      "parentUuid": "c5502c68_66591b90",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 4750,
        "startChar": 35,
        "endLine": 4750,
        "endChar": 40
      },
      "revId": "861ab4bffcf8847cf5277ed5b722d6ed445365aa",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "024d8e2a_38da4720",
        "filename": "include/ec_commands.h",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 4750,
      "author": {
        "id": 1442602
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-01-31T18:26:47Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Got it, ok so let me add Guenter to the discussion as he has reviewed kernel part on the kernel side",
      "parentUuid": "50b1544a_10228eb6",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 4750,
        "startChar": 35,
        "endLine": 4750,
        "endChar": 40
      },
      "revId": "861ab4bffcf8847cf5277ed5b722d6ed445365aa",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "7f763e47_0315dcb0",
        "filename": "include/ec_commands.h",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 4750,
      "author": {
        "id": 1146659
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-01-31T18:45:33Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "That is not for the driver to decide. The maximum timeout should be the maximum _supported_ timeout, not some random number determined by the developer. Frankly, I am kind of lost here. Sure, a lower timeout will potentially catch more problems. What prevents the user (us) from setting such a lower timeout ? What does that have to do with the maximum timeout that is supported ?\nFWIW, the minimum timeout should be the minimum timeout supported by the EC. It should not be \"the lowest timeout that I think makes sense\". If the EC supports a minimum timeout of 1 second, that should be the limit to use here. Again, the actual timeout is decided by the user, not by the author of the watchdog driver.\n\nIf that isn\u0027t clear enough: If you set artificial (not limited by EC) limits for minimum and maximum linits, you are abusing the watchdog kernel API. I might consider NACKing the driver if you are doing that.",
      "parentUuid": "024d8e2a_38da4720",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 4750,
        "startChar": 35,
        "endLine": 4750,
        "endChar": 40
      },
      "revId": "861ab4bffcf8847cf5277ed5b722d6ed445365aa",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "23460c7e_7dd9c71d",
        "filename": "include/ec_commands.h",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 4750,
      "author": {
        "id": 1442602
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-01-31T19:32:04Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "For now EC code limits the minimum timeout to 5 seconds (https://chromium.googlesource.com/chromiumos/platform/ec/+/refs/heads/main/common/ap_hang_detect.c#79) and the max limit is set by the size of the `reboot_timeout_sec` field (uint16_t).",
      "parentUuid": "7f763e47_0315dcb0",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 4750,
        "startChar": 35,
        "endLine": 4750,
        "endChar": 40
      },
      "revId": "861ab4bffcf8847cf5277ed5b722d6ed445365aa",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "b4586623_4dddc524",
        "filename": "include/ec_commands.h",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 4750,
      "author": {
        "id": 1395927
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-02-01T13:29:05Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Minimal is design decision to prevent booting loop, enforced in EC code.\nMaximal is delivered from maximal value that can be stored in u16 protocol field.\nThis discussion suggest, both should have those mentioned in comments of *.h file to clarify similar questions IMO.",
      "parentUuid": "23460c7e_7dd9c71d",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 4750,
        "startChar": 35,
        "endLine": 4750,
        "endChar": 40
      },
      "revId": "861ab4bffcf8847cf5277ed5b722d6ed445365aa",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "bc6f7538_354aa51b",
        "filename": "include/ec_commands.h",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 4750,
      "author": {
        "id": 1395927
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-02-01T13:32:10Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "If you do not like comment, instead of 65535 which might be perceived as magic use U16_MAX or similar. Problem is this those defines are different in kernel and EC and this header is used in both.",
      "revId": "861ab4bffcf8847cf5277ed5b722d6ed445365aa",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "53778105_ace430c0",
        "filename": "include/ec_commands.h",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 4750,
      "author": {
        "id": 1146659
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-02-01T15:03:31Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The enforced limits should hopefully be the same in both.",
      "parentUuid": "bc6f7538_354aa51b",
      "revId": "861ab4bffcf8847cf5277ed5b722d6ed445365aa",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "4ff62232_47216fe3",
        "filename": "include/ec_commands.h",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 4750,
      "author": {
        "id": 1146659
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-02-01T15:03:31Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The watchdog kernel API says \"the watchdog timer\u0027s {minimum, maximum} timeout value\". It doesn\u0027t say \"the {minimum, maximum} timeout thought to be reasonable by the driver developer\". I think that is defined well enough, or at least well enough that it was never questioned up to now. Also, if there is a \"thought to be reasonable\" maximum timeout, it seems to me that it should be enforced by the watchdog core, not on a per driver basis (not that I have any idea what such a maximum timeout might be).",
      "parentUuid": "b4586623_4dddc524",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 4750,
        "startChar": 35,
        "endLine": 4750,
        "endChar": 40
      },
      "revId": "861ab4bffcf8847cf5277ed5b722d6ed445365aa",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
